        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "bfd1e1a1-9b64-5692-9895-c59f1fbfaba8",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Lola Dee, Johnnie Ray, Bob Hope, Jimmy Durante, Tony Bennett\n- Companies: Mercury Records, Columbia Records\n- Organizations: Junior Junction\n- Roles: American singer, recording artist, songwriter, pianist\n- Concepts: rock and roll\n\nFacts:\n- John Alvin Ray was born on January 10, 1927, and he became an American singer, songwriter, and pianist.\n- Lola Dee was heard in an amateur contest at age 14 and was asked to audition for the network teen-aged show \"Junior Junction.\"\n- Lola Dee signed a recording contract at age 16.\n- Johnnie Ray was extremely popular for most of the 1950s.\n- Lola Dee was an American singer and recording artist associated with Mercury Records and Columbia Records in the 1950s and 1960s.\n- In 1955, Lola Dee recorded the half-million-selling song \"Only You\" as part of more than 40 recorded sides.\n- In the late 1950s and 1960s, Lola Dee toured with stars including Bob Hope, Johnnie Ray, and Jimmy Durante.\n- Critics cited Johnnie Ray as a major precursor to rock and roll because of his jazz- and blues-influenced music and animated stage personality.\n- Tony Bennett called Johnnie Ray the \"father of rock and roll,\" and historians noted him as a pioneering figure in the genre's development.\n- John Alvin Ray died on February 24, 1990.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "ec5042ca-e633-5d0d-a0b4-6d358879b67c",
        "text": "Page: Lola Dee. Sentence: Lola Dee is an American singer and recording artist with Mercury Records and Columbia Records labels in the 1950s and 1960s.\nPage: Lola Dee. Sentence: At the age of 14, she was heard in an amateur contest and asked to audition for a network teen-aged show called \"Junior Junction\".\nPage: Lola Dee. Sentence: At 16 she was signed to a recording contract.\nPage: Lola Dee. Sentence: She recorded over 40 sides, including the half million best seller \"Only You\" in 1955.\nPage: Lola Dee. Sentence: Her popularity as a recording artist gave her the opportunity to tour with such stars as Bob Hope, Johnnie Ray and Jimmy Durante in the late 1950s and 1960s.\nPage: Johnnie Ray. Sentence: John Alvin Ray (January 10, 1927 – February 24, 1990) was an American singer, songwriter, and pianist.\nPage: Johnnie Ray. Sentence: Extremely popular for most of the 1950s, Ray has been cited by critics as a major precursor to what would become rock and roll, for his jazz and blues-influenced music and his animated stage personality.\nPage: Johnnie Ray. Sentence: Tony Bennett called Ray the \"father of rock and roll,\" and historians have noted him as a pioneering figure in the development of the genre.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
